{"kind":"report","text":"Bounded assessment for request402 — proposed authenticated segmented full-prefix recovery contract. Recommended for a separately commissioned implementation/independent review, NOT adopted and NOT a G2/G5 pass.\n\nThe smallest preserving design is to divide the plaintext ordered history into sealed, nonempty bounded intervals, encrypt each interval with a fresh independent key, and link their owner-signed descriptors into one constant-size named checkpoint. Keep a separately encrypted owner vault with live state, not another copy of the whole history. Reuse sealed intervals and the immutable definition closure when appending. Encrypting one giant export and cutting only its ciphertext into network pieces does not solve the128-entry limit, key custody, client memory or proof of full history. Rewriting/encrypting every prior prefix at every checkpoint is unnecessary quadratic work. A forward-derived shared key chain would grant future access; independent random segment keys avoid introducing a new derivation construction.\n\nScope/evidence: this assessment reasons from the immutable398ccbe6bf6fae3a81dc646298aba0066aa2c042 source reviewed in actual report400/a45d17fa552a82a70dd1dde4b88298fa8b30bacc, Plan001, ratified direction363, and the primary sources below. I reread the frozen archive/crypto/wire/contracts rather than adopting builder changes as the design. Existing actual P1c evidence establishes small full-prefix archives, per-message openings, separate vault recovery and local gateway interfaces; it does NOT execute the segmented construction proposed here. No new composition experiments or source edits were performed. As instructed, I paused this assessment to complete fresh corrected-candidate review437, reporting7400fd46e78682646aa802bb8f744562f5dd4238. Its ordinary retention/readback failure findings P1C-04/05 must be corrected independently; this proposal does not duplicate that work or waive its obligations.\n\nSource motivation: frozen archive.ts:41–50 caps creation at128 entries and builds one full body/checkpoint;57–87 verifies contiguous full-prefix entries and openings, including a repeated openings.filter per entry;88–106 exports/restores a vault containing the whole Snapshot. crypto.ts:103–105 restarts from one serialized snapshot, and146 clones the entire accumulated data on each receive. Those are bounded small-fixture implementations, not evidence of linear longer-history packaging or persistent-client behavior. Frozen confidentiality-and-recovery.md:18–66 explicitly distinguishes real application AEAD/signature checks from owner-attested control/public-context validity over genesis through the named tip. Plan001:149–150 sets128KiB events,32KiB acts,256MiB journal/64MiB outbox and no eviction of acknowledged required history;634,677 retain100/1,000/10,000-event measurements. The proposal preserves those targets and full fold-to-UI/direct JSON or typed-array projections; it requires no SQL materialization.\n\nProposed contract G2-S01 — exact intervals and a cycle-free identity graph.\nUse a NEW explicitly versioned recovery profile/format; do not silently reinterpret owner-attested-full-prefix@1 or its checkpoint-bearing AES wrapper. Retain current context tuple {instance,genesis,definition,owner,sequencer}, strict canonical encoding, signature/ID rules and existing pinned crypto. Freeze exact field names/domains before implementing the vectors.\n\nEach segment contains ONLY its interval's original ordered events (and embedded signed submissions/admissions), original signed application actions and message-specific openings. It carries the start/end public admission state or authenticated state hashes and necessary public GroupContext/tree material, without private epoch/ratchet/leaf secrets. A small dictionary may deduplicate identical public context/tree bytes within a segment. Do not embed prior segments/full-prefix snapshots in every segment.\n\nSeal at the first of128 ordered entries or16MiB canonical plaintext. Count every ordered entry, including control, stale and deterministic refusal entries, separately from the number of non-stale application messages/openings, decoded action bytes, canonical plaintext, encrypted bytes and total signed transport bytes. No empty segments. If adding the next entry would exceed a segment bound, seal the prior nonempty interval and begin the next. A single entry plus mandatory opening/context that exceeds the segment envelope refuses explicitly; it never disappears from promised coverage. A stalled/invalid-crypto suffix cannot be called recovered merely because its outer signed order is retained.\n\nUse this acyclic construction:\n1. BodyCore is the canonical interval payload and boundary states, with no self-hash or future checkpoint ID.\n2. SegmentMeta commits the exact context/profile, ordinal, inclusive first/last position, predecessor original-entry ID, final original-entry ID, previous SegmentManifest ID (null only for the first), entry/application counts, before/after public-state hashes, definition ID, BodyCore hash, plaintext/ciphertext lengths, fresh random key ID and nonce. Its domain-separated hash is metaId.\n3. Encrypt BodyCore once under a new random32-byte AES-256-GCM key,12-byte random nonce and128-bit tag, with exact SegmentMeta/domain as AAD. Split the resulting ciphertext into bounded16KiB transport chunks. Each ordinary signed chunk binds context,metaId,index,total and exact bytes. This second split is transport chunking, not the history segmentation itself.\n4. Sign an owner SegmentManifest containing SegmentMeta, the ordered exact chunk event IDs and total ciphertext hash/length. Manifest ID is deliberately NOT in earlier chunk/AAD inputs. The current128KiB signed-event cap remains; a16MiB plaintext plus tag needs at most1,025 such chunks, so its ID list appears feasible under the event cap, but exact encoded max/+1 must be measured rather than assumed.\n5. Finally sign the overall FullPrefixCheckpoint: explicit full-history type/profile/context, monotonic owner checkpoint counter and previous checkpoint ID, last SegmentManifest ID, segment count, total entry/application/encoded-byte counts, coverage start1/endT, exact sequencer-signed tipT, immutable encrypted definition descriptor ID, and compact owner-vault plaintext hash. No field commits its own ID. The complete checkpoint commits the descriptor chain through its last manifest; appending one segment does not rewrite all previous descriptors.\n\nA reverse-linked descriptor chain is enough for full-history recovery; a general Merkle/range-proof service is not required now. A reader can discover/validate the chain backwards into a bounded compact index or spool, then replay forwards. Full-prefix verification is linear; random seek and resumable fetching can be added later without changing sealed identities. Flat signed manifests are acceptable for a one-off static file but repeatedly reissuing a list of every segment at each append recreates quadratic metadata work.\n\nG2-S02 — whole-prefix verification and what is actually trusted.\nRequire the first interval to start1 and reference the trusted genesis predecessor. Require exact ordinal increments, next.first=previous.last+1, next.predecessor=previous.finalEntryID and exact previous manifest ID. Reject gaps, overlap, duplicate manifests/positions, cycles, reordering, foreign context, substitution, zero-length intervals and mismatched total counts/end tip. Verify every original entry's real signature/position/predecessor/context and the original ciphertext submission; a signed list of hashes alone is not a recovered history.\n\nCarry one public control/epoch state across segments. Derive each next state with the current signed owner admission rules; compare segment before state with the prior derived after state and final state with the checkpoint/vault. Segments may cross epochs or start/end with a control; they are not independently initialized MLS groups. Every non-stale application position has exactly one correctly paired opening, including genuine sender-data/content AEAD, full codec/padding, MLS signature and original account/action/leaf correspondence. There are no extra openings or substituted valid-but-unrelated actions. Carry a global bounded logical-action map across segment boundaries: repeated identical logical/action IDs have the existing duplicate disposition, while conflicting signed actions under one logical ID halt; replay must never apply a duplicate twice.\n\nPreserve the explicit G2 trust statement: the owner attests historical encrypted control-transition validity and the associated public contexts over the ENTIRE genesis-to-T interval, composed through the signed descriptor chain/checkpoint. This does not become independent historical MLS Commit replay. Applications still undergo the genuine cryptographic correspondence checks already demonstrated; a dishonest immutable owner still has the stated attestation power. A checkpoint signing secret alone does not derive independently random archive keys; physical key-role reuse, if any, must be described separately.\n\nRetain one immutable salted definition/asset closure, bound to the genesis definition ID, with the existing64-file/512KiB decoded bound. Encrypt it separately under an independent definition key and use its signed descriptor from all checkpoints/segments. Do not duplicate the entire closure in each interval. The client checks sorted unique paths, traversal/codec rules, exact files and original salted commitment; the relay sees only the allowed public commitment/wrapper metadata.\n\nThe caller supplies independently trusted owner/genesis and, when freshness or full promised coverage matters, the expected checkpoint/tip. A valid old checkpoint is a valid OLD complete prefix, never a substitute for expected newer C/T. Signed counters, previous-checkpoint links, NIP timestamps or EOSE cannot prove absence of an unseen later checkpoint/removal. Ambiguous owner-signed forks are surfaced/stall according to the existing policy rather than selecting a convenient shorter root.\n\nG2-S03 — keys, owner vault and static versus incremental exports.\nA shared segment key is scoped to one SEALED immutable interval. Never append later plaintext under a segment key already delivered to a history holder; this would expose later additions even within the same MLS epoch. Exact retries use the original ciphertext/nonce bytes. Re-encrypting changed content requires a new independent key/nonce and new descriptor; it is not an overwrite of the old object. Appending creates only a new interval/key/descriptor and a new checkpoint, preserving every previous segment identity.\n\nFor full-newcomer recovery, deliver the exact keys for the checkpoint's segment chain plus the definition key, with no extra/future segment key and no continuing MLS/ratchet/owner-vault key. Avoid thousands of keys inside a NIP44 message. Use a bounded encrypted snapshot-specific key-grant bundle mapping [segmentManifestID,key] (plus definition key), under a fresh random grant key. A small signed Nostr delivery event carries a NIP44-encrypted grant key and exact recipient/context/checkpoint/grant-locator binding. Verify the outer event's authorized sender, identity and signature before NIP44 decryption. Keep the current small key-packet budget or measure/freeze its adjusted envelope; do not infer NIP44 protocol conformance from calling an encrypt helper alone. The pinned NIP44 explicitly requires the signed-event context and validation before decrypting: https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb/44.md .\n\nThe grant bundle belongs to one checkpoint. A new checkpoint gets a NEW grant key/bundle if it adds keys; do not append future keys to a bundle encrypted under a grant key given to an old holder. Existing authorized members may receive a small exact new-segment grant. All keys in a full grant are checked against the checkpoint chain and per-segment key IDs, including duplicate/extra/wrong-definition/foreign-context rejection.\n\nThe owner recovery vault also needs composition: merely splitting the shared archive while retaining a giant embedded Snapshot in every owner export leaves all-device-loss recovery bounded by the old whole-file representation. Propose a settled-prefix-only compact VaultCore containing exact context/tip/archive-head, accepted serialized MLS state, account/device/leaf bindings and keys, final public state, and only non-derivable private continuation state. Reconstruct historical events/openings/outcomes/logical/published indexes from the verified archive instead of copying all history into VaultCore. Public/history index derivation must be exact, not trusted installation of a supplied snapshot.\n\nOne cycle-free custody order is: seal history manifests; prepare bounded GrantCore with context/archive-head and exact key map (no future checkpoint ID), and choose its fresh grant key; prepare VaultCore containing that key plus GrantCore hash and live state; create checkpoint committing VaultCore hash and archive-head; then encrypt both GrantCore and VaultCore with headers/AAD bound to the checkpoint, using DIFFERENT independent grant/vault keys. Sign their locators after those ciphertexts exist. The checkpoint does not also reference those later ciphertext IDs. The grant recipient gets only the grant key. The all-device-loss package explicitly retains checkpoint, all segments/closure, encrypted grant, encrypted vault and separately held vault key; it needs no lost live object. It decrypts/validates the vault/core bindings but installs private continuation state only after the whole archive, final roster/epoch/leaf and checkpoint relationships verify. Exact final keys/MLS state and pending=null must agree with the settled checkpoint.\n\nThis preserves existing limits: copied old shared keys/openings decrypt their old interval forever; rewrapping does not revoke those copies. Fresh future segment keys and actual MLS removal bound future access under the uncompromised-key assumption. A stolen old owner vault remains stronger, especially if it includes the immutable owner authority; this scheme adds no owner transfer or secure-erasure guarantee. NIP44 itself supplies no forward/post-compromise secrecy. MLS recovery/export changes must not be described as strengthening RFC9420's deletion assumptions: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9420.html .\n\nStatic exports are complete only after the exact checkpoint's entire closure is available. Incremental sealing retains old immutable objects, adds only new ranges and updates the constant-size checkpoint. Keep an unsealed interval private/buffered; it cannot be advertised as complete or have its key issued. For a current full newcomer checkpoint, seal exactly at that accepted join boundary. The minimal profile supports full checkpoints only at sealed interval ends; a mirror ending inside a later segment must use a prior available sealed checkpoint and label the rest unavailable, or obtain a properly sealed checkpoint at its frontier. It must not release a segment extending beyond its authorization frontier just because the requester asked for an earlier tip.\n\nG2-S04 — gateway boundary and minimal transport now.\nKeep three notions separate: F, the gateway's monotonically highest verified retained authorization prefix; T, the requested full archive checkpoint; and the complete object closure actually retained for T. Serve only T\u003c=F and only manifests/chunks whose authenticated declared coverage ends no later thanT. An older data cursor/checkpoint cannot lower authorization F. Learned newer valid revocation, gap/fork/restore fences and each dequeue/send membership check apply to every descriptor/chunk page as they do to ordinary history. The accepted unseen-removal old-history exposure remains explicit; segmentation does not repair global freshness or erase old capabilities.\n\nWithout keys the gateway can verify ordinary signatures/IDs, owner/genesis/context, manifest links/range/count declarations, correspondence to its original contiguous journal/tip, exact chunk identities/indexes/ciphertext sizes and complete declared retention. It cannot verify private BodyCore hashes, plaintext definition contents, original-action openings or independently prove historical MLS control validity. Those are client/attestation checks. Owner-signed false manifests cannot be advertised as client-verified recovery merely because public retention matches them.\n\nThe public interface should fetch bounded exact manifest/chunk IDs under one checkpoint and prefix authorization, without raw backend passthrough. Keep128 events/1MiB pages and131,200-byte frames; do not flatten a larger closure into the existing single-page NOSEQ-CLOSURE response. A missing/wrong object is partial/unavailable; EOSE is not full-prefix completion. Deny alternate COUNT/NEG/admin paths and require private backend/network separation unchanged. Bind authorization and declared coverage at the actual send boundary, including removed readers requesting old pages.\n\nBefore composition adoption, one SMALL real gateway/strfry fixture must transport at least two nonempty archive intervals, at least two authenticated chunks/pages, a signed whole checkpoint and complete newcomer grant. A real newly admitted client must retrieve through the gateway and perform full private verification/decryption. Exercise one missing chunk, one correctly signed but wrongly bound object/manifest, one attempted interval beyondF and a revocation between page deliveries; older cursor retries stay denied. This establishes a viable multi-part interface now without implementing production download scheduling, restartable resume, independent operations or P5 durability. The actual P1c readback/reservation/fence findings must be resolved rather than inherited into this fixture.\n\nG2-S05 — explicit bounds, work and admission honesty.\nProcess one bounded segment (plus its ciphertext/base64 allocation), one bounded definition closure and one public/MLS state at a time. A compact descriptor index/spool and global logical map may grow only under explicit total bounds. Use indexed opening lookup, not an unbounded full-history filter per entry. Iterate/emit canonical records without concatenating/copying every prior prefix; source and verifier should expose iterators or equivalent bounded slices. Packaging and verification target O(total encoded bytes + per-entry crypto + segment/chunk metadata), not O(N²). A one-shot WebCrypto AES-GCM call still holds a full segment and temporary copies; measure peak memory, do not call it streaming AEAD. NIST's GCM specification supports authenticated additional data but does not make this application framing/retention contract automatic: https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/38/d/final .\n\nSuggested explicit total safety dimensions for the NEXT FEASIBILITY PROFILE, subject to root acceptance before adoption: at most65,536 ordered entries and at most65,536 nonempty sealed segments (including singleton checkpoints); a16MiB canonical full key-grant plaintext cap with compact [64-hex manifest ID,64-hex key] pairs; a bounded compact descriptor index/spool with checked allocation; and the EXISTING256MiB aggregate required retained signed bytes plus64MiB unpublished outbox. Entry/segment counts are new proposed safety ceilings, not secretly adopted service limits or a128-entry lifetime.65,536 compact key pairs fit below16MiB before their bounded fixed metadata, but exact encoded proof/max+1 remains required. A different justified cap is a genuine acceptance choice; it must preserve the10,000-small-event target. Array counts, cumulative byte sums, integer overflow, total fetch work and declared lengths are validated before allocation/fetch. Total retention counts orders, segment/chunk/manifests, definition/required grant closure and superseded required roots rather than only application plaintext.\n\n10,000 maximal32KiB actions cannot all fit256MiB: the frozen actual ordered wrapper measurement was46,251 bytes at the max action, so ordered events alone would exceed462MB. Plan001's10,000-event benchmark is a bounded-small-state/workload target, not a guarantee that every maximal act fits. Define representative small acts and report actual encoded totals; stop on the first applicable count/byte/outbox limit without evicting acknowledged history.\n\nRefusal must not promise an unrecoverable newer prefix. Freeze an admission/storage eligibility rule that reserves a conservative bounded allowance for required archive/manifest/grant closure (and uncertain writes) before an ACK claims that stronger recoverable boundary. Reservation can use actual prepared sizes or a justified upper bound derived from encoded inputs and bounded proof/context overhead; an opaque sequencer cannot magically know successful private decryption. Distinguish an outer ordered receipt from verified/exported/retained full-history coverage atT. On closure-capacity or crypto failure, keep the last completeT explicit and reject/withhold the stronger newer promise; never label a shortened prefix complete for the requested newer checkpoint. The exact eligibility/reservation rule is a BEFORE-ADOPTION contract choice. Production transaction/outbox implementation remains P4/P5; the bounded fail/refusal state-machine vector is needed now.\n\nG2-S06 — minimal discriminating executable evidence before adoption.\n1. Actual Node AND Chromium: at least three nonempty sealed intervals, more than128 total ordered entries, at least two epoch changes, with controls both inside intervals and at a boundary. Include authentic applications, deterministic refusal and an exact re-encryption duplicate crossing intervals. Verify original order/signatures, all actual openings, final public state and identical complete synthetic projection. A fresh newcomer joins at the current checkpoint via actual Welcome, receives the bounded full grant and recovers every promised prior application.\n2. Declared all-device-loss: retain only the specified package and separately held vault key, discard all previous live client objects, reconstruct history/indexes, restore final accepted MLS/device state and send a new real message a retained peer accepts. Mismatched vault/checkpoint/epoch/leaf fails before installation.\n3. Exact coverage/root negatives: missing/misordered/duplicated/truncated/overlapping/cyclic/substituted segments; wrong original predecessor/end tip; wrong genesis/owner/definition/checkpoint/key; wrong key mapping or extra future key; stale but internally valid complete prefix against an expected newer checkpoint; extra/missing/duplicated openings and cross-segment logical conflict. Re-sign mutations with the fixture owner where necessary so tests reach inner invariants rather than all failing only at a superficial hash.\n4. Parser/capacity: malformed/unknown/duplicate fields, unsafe integers/index/count/lengths, noncanonical encodings, signed-but-inconsistent manifests, altered AEAD AAD/ciphertext/tag.128 entries then129 forms two valid intervals, rather than global refusal. Exact segment plaintext max/+1 rolls to another segment; single-entry oversize refuses. Test exact signed-manifest/chunk/frame/grant/aggregate/count limits and+1, including refusal before a false newer completeness promise.\n5. Custody/append: earlier sealed ciphertext/manifests remain byte-identical on append; fresh independent new segment and new full-grant keys; old segment/grant keys cannot decrypt a later sealed interval even when appended in the same MLS epoch. An actually removed history holder receives its valid removal Commit and cannot decrypt future MLS data; a retained-member/non-removal catch-up positive control succeeds. Rewrapping demonstrably leaves old copied openings usable; shared grants never contain vault/private MLS continuation material.\n6. Small actual multi-page gateway test specified inS04, plus interrupted sealing/transfer cannot emit a full-complete result before all closure bytes are present. Record source/profile/native identities, actual cases/raw reports, retained failures and negative verifier probes. Instrument maximum simultaneously decoded segments and small increasing workloads to catch full-prefix copying/re-encryption; the10,000-event performance run itself remains P6/P9.\n\nDecision recommended now: authorize a narrow proposed-profile composition fixture implementingS01–S06, with fresh independent exact-head review afterward. Do not activate gates or production adoption from this report. The genuine choices to freeze are (a) exact v2 canonical schemas/acyclic commitments and sealed-boundary checkpoint policy, (b) total safety caps plus recoverability/retention reservation eligibility, and (c) explicit later reaffirmation of owner-attested historical trust, separate key custody and named-prefix unseen-revocation exposure. The recommendation is independent keys and linked immutable descriptors, not a128-entry instance lifetime. Resumable network download, durable production vault/restore,10,000-event timing, generic fold execution/UI and final deployment stay in their existing P5/P6/P9 owners.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true"}}